/**
  @page examples-index Examples and tutorials
  @brief Examples utilizing the Rodin library

  @tableofcontents

  This page provides a comprehensive guide to the example programs shipped with
  Rodin. Each section explains the mathematical problem, walks through the code,
  and highlights the Rodin features used. Examples are the best way to learn how
  to use the library — we recommend reading them alongside the
  @ref guides-index "guides".

  @section examples-index-gallery Gallery

  For a visual overview of what Rodin can do, visit the @ref gallery "Gallery".


  @section examples-index-pdes Solving PDEs

  These examples demonstrate the core workflow: create a mesh, build a finite
  element space, define trial/test functions, compose a @ref Rodin::Variational::Problem
  "Problem" from integrals and boundary conditions, solve, and save results.

  @subsection examples-index-pdes-poisson Poisson Equation

  **File:** `examples/PDEs/Poisson.cpp`

  **Problem:**
  @f[
    -\Delta u = 1 \quad \text{in } \Omega = [0,1]^2, \qquad u = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega.
  @f]

  **Full code:**
  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, { 16, 16 });
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2); // Compute boundary

  P1 vh(mesh);

  TrialFunction u(vh);
  TestFunction  v(vh);

  RealFunction f = 1;

  Problem poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
          - Integral(f, v)
          + DirichletBC(u, Zero());
  CG(poisson).solve();

  IO::XDMF xdmf("Poisson");
  xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("u", u.getSolution());
  xdmf.write();
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh::UniformGrid "Mesh::UniformGrid" — structured
    triangular grid factory
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::P1 "P1" — piecewise-linear finite element space
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::Integral "Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))" — the
    bilinear form @f$ \int_\Omega \nabla u \cdot \nabla v @f$
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::DirichletBC "DirichletBC(u, Zero())" — homogeneous
    Dirichlet on the full boundary (no `.on()` call needed)
  - `CG(poisson).solve()` — inline solver pattern: constructs, solves, and
    discards the conjugate-gradient solver in one expression
  - @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "IO::XDMF" with `grid().setMesh(mesh).add(...)` —
    chained XDMF output for ParaView visualization
  - `mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2)` — computes edge-to-face incidence,
    required for boundary detection

  @subsection examples-index-pdes-elasticity Linear Elasticity

  **File:** `examples/PDEs/Elasticity.cpp`

  **Problem:** Find the displacement @f$ \mathbf{u} @f$ of an elastic body
  with Lamé parameters @f$ \lambda, \mu @f$:
  @f[
    -\nabla \cdot \sigma(\mathbf{u}) = 0, \qquad
    \sigma = \lambda (\operatorname{tr}\varepsilon) I + 2\mu\varepsilon, \qquad
    \varepsilon = \tfrac{1}{2}(\nabla\mathbf{u} + \nabla\mathbf{u}^T).
  @f]
  Dirichlet (fixed) boundary on @f$ \Gamma_D @f$, Neumann (traction) on
  @f$ \Gamma_N @f$.

  **Key code:**
  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh.load(meshFile, IO::FileFormat::MFEM);
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2);

  size_t d = mesh.getSpaceDimension();
  P1 fes(mesh, d);  // vector-valued P1 space

  const Real lambda = 0.5769, mu = 0.3846;
  VectorFunction f{0, -1};  // downward pull

  TrialFunction u(fes);
  TestFunction  v(fes);

  Problem elasticity(u, v);
  elasticity = LinearElasticityIntegral(u, v)(lambda, mu)
             - BoundaryIntegral(f, v).over(GammaN)
             + DirichletBC(u, VectorFunction{0, 0}).on(GammaD);
  Solver::CG(elasticity).solve();
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - `P1(mesh, d)` — vector-valued FE space with `d` components
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::LinearElasticityIntegral
    "LinearElasticityIntegral(u, v)(lambda, mu)" — built-in integrator that
    assembles @f$ \int_\Omega 2\mu\,\varepsilon(u):\varepsilon(v) +
    \lambda\,(\operatorname{div}u)(\operatorname{div}v) @f$
  - `BoundaryIntegral(f, v).over(GammaN)` — Neumann traction restricted to
    a boundary attribute
  - `DirichletBC(u, ...).on(GammaD)` — Dirichlet BC on a specific boundary
    attribute
  - `VectorFunction{0, -1}` — vector constant from initializer list
  - `mesh.load(file, IO::FileFormat::MFEM)` — loading external mesh files
  - @ref Rodin::Alert::Info "Alert::Info()" stream pattern for logging

  @subsection examples-index-pdes-helmholtz Helmholtz Equation (Complex-valued)

  **File:** `examples/PDEs/Helmholtz.cpp`

  **Problem:** Complex-valued Helmholtz equation:
  @f[
    -\Delta u - k^2 u = f \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = u_{\mathrm{ex}} \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega.
  @f]

  **Key code:**
  @code{.cpp}
  P1<Complex> vh(mesh);     // complex-valued P1 space

  TrialFunction u(vh);
  TestFunction  v(vh);

  ComplexFunction f = [&](const Point& p) { return Complex(p.x(), 1) * ...; };
  ComplexFunction u_ex = [&](const Point& p) { ... };

  Problem helmholtz(u, v);
  helmholtz = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
            - waveNumber * waveNumber * Integral(u, v)
            - Integral(f, v)
            + DirichletBC(u, u_ex);
  CG(helmholtz).solve();

  // Extract real and imaginary parts
  P1<Real> rh(mesh);
  GridFunction uRe(rh);
  GridFunction uIm(rh);
  uRe = Re(u.getSolution());
  uIm = Im(u.getSolution());
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - `P1<Complex>` — complex scalar finite element space
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::ComplexFunction "ComplexFunction" from lambda
  - `Re(u)` and `Im(u)` — real/imaginary part extraction operators
  - The `- k^2 * Integral(u, v)` mass term for the reaction operator
  - Complex Dirichlet boundary conditions

  @subsection examples-index-pdes-periodic Periodic Boundary Conditions

  **File:** `examples/PDEs/Periodic.cpp`

  **Problem:** Poisson equation on @f$ [0, 2\pi]^2 @f$ with periodic
  boundary conditions linking left↔right and bottom↔top DOFs.

  **Key code:**
  @code{.cpp}
  IndexMap<IndexSet> dofs;
  for (Index i = 0; i < vh.getSize(); i += n)
    dofs[i].insert(i + n - 1);     // left ↔ right
  for (Index i = 0; i < n; i++)
    dofs[i].insert(i + n * (n - 1)); // bottom ↔ top

  Problem poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
          - Integral(f, v)
          + PeriodicBC(u, dofs);
  Solver::SparseLU(poisson).solve();
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::PeriodicBC "PeriodicBC(u, dofs)" — periodic
    boundary conditions via DOF identification maps
  - `IndexMap<IndexSet>` — maps each primary DOF to a set of secondary DOFs
    that must have the same value
  - `Solver::SparseLU` — direct sparse solver (Eigen's SparseLU)

  @subsection examples-index-pdes-surfacemesh Surface PDE

  **File:** `examples/PDEs/SurfaceMesh.cpp`

  **Problem:** Reaction-diffusion on a curved surface
  @f$ \Gamma \subset \mathbb{R}^3 @f$:
  @f[
    -\Delta_\Gamma u + u = f \quad \text{on } \Gamma.
  @f]

  **Key code:**
  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh Omega;
  Omega.load(meshFile, IO::FileFormat::MFEM); // surface mesh in R^3

  H1 Vh(Omega); // H1 order 1 on a surface mesh

  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction  v(Vh);

  Problem rd(u, v);
  rd = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
     + Integral(u, v)
     - Integral(f, v);
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - Surface meshes (mesh dimension < space dimension) work transparently
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::H1 "H1" space on a surface mesh — Rodin
    handles the surface Jacobian internally
  - Reaction-diffusion: combination of stiffness `Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))`
    and mass `Integral(u, v)` terms

  @section examples-index-dg Discontinuous Galerkin

  **File:** `examples/DG/Poisson.cpp`

  **Problem:** Poisson equation solved with the Symmetric Interior Penalty (SIP)
  DG method using piecewise-constant (@ref Rodin::Variational::P0 "P0") elements.

  **Full code:**
  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, 16, 16);
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2);

  P0 vh(mesh);   // piecewise-constant DG space

  TrialFunction u(vh);
  TestFunction  v(vh);

  RealFunction f = 1.0;
  RealFunction g = 0.0;

  Problem poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
          - Integral(f, v)
          + DirichletBC(u, g);

  Solver::SparseLU solver;
  poisson.solve(solver);
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::P0 "P0" — discontinuous piecewise-constant FE
    space (1 DOF per cell)
  - `Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))` with P0 functions — Rodin's DG assembly
    automatically handles the interior penalty terms
  - `poisson.solve(solver)` — the second solver pattern: create a reusable
    solver, then pass it to `Problem::solve()`
  - `SparseLU` is preferred for DG problems because the resulting system is
    often non-symmetric

  @see @ref guides-variational "Variational Formulations Guide" for the
  mathematical DG formulation and additional operators (`Jump`, `Average`,
  `FaceIntegral`, `InterfaceIntegral`).

  @section examples-index-io Input/Output

  @subsection examples-index-io-xdmf XDMF Time Series

  **File:** `examples/IO/XDMF.cpp`

  **Problem:** Export a time-evolving vector field to XDMF for visualization in
  ParaView.

  **Key code:**
  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf("TimeEvolution");

  auto grid = xdmf.grid();
  grid.setMesh(mesh);
  grid.add(u);  // register the vector field

  for (size_t k = 0; k < Nt; ++k)
  {
    const Real t = k * dt;
    u = [t](const Geometry::Point& p) { ... };  // update field
    xdmf.write(t);                               // export snapshot
  }
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - `xdmf.grid()` returns a Grid handle for the default grid
  - `grid.setMesh(mesh)` binds a mesh to the grid; `grid.add(u)` registers
    fields — both return `Grid&` for method chaining
  - `xdmf.write(t)` writes a timestamped snapshot of all registered fields
  - The field data is read from the @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction
    "GridFunction" at each `write()` call, so updating the grid function and
    calling `write()` again captures the new state
  - `xdmf.write(t).flush()` ensures data is flushed to disk immediately

  @subsection examples-index-io-formats Mesh Format Conversion

  **File:** `examples/IO/MEDIT.cpp`

  Converts a MEDIT mesh to MFEM format:
  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh.load(argv[1], IO::FileFormat::MEDIT);
  mesh.save("mfem.mesh", IO::FileFormat::MFEM);
  @endcode

  Supported formats: @ref Rodin::IO::FileFormat "MFEM", MEDIT, HDF5, and
  @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "XDMF".

  - @subpage examples-io-index

  @section examples-index-geometry Mesh Geometry

  @subsection examples-index-geometry-box Box Surface Meshes

  **File:** `examples/Geometry/Box.cpp`

  `Mesh::Box` creates a **surface mesh** (boundary of a box) where the face
  type determines the embedding dimension:

  @code{.cpp}
  mesh = mesh.Box(Polytope::Type::Point, { n });            // 0D points on 1D line
  mesh = mesh.Box(Polytope::Type::Segment, { n, n });       // 1D edges on 2D square boundary
  mesh = mesh.Box(Polytope::Type::Triangle, { n, n, n });   // 2D triangles on 3D cube boundary
  mesh = mesh.Box(Polytope::Type::Quadrilateral, { n, n, n }); // 2D quads on 3D cube boundary
  @endcode

  @note `Box` takes a **face type** (not a cell type) and produces a surface
  mesh where the spatial dimension is one higher than the mesh dimension.

  @subsection examples-index-geometry-normal Boundary Normals

  **File:** `examples/Variational/Normal.cpp`

  Projects the outward unit normal onto a displaced mesh:
  @code{.cpp}
  mesh.displace(VectorFunction{ 0, 0.2 * sin(2 * M_PI * F::x) });

  P1 fes(mesh, vdim);
  GridFunction gf(fes);
  gf.project(Region::Boundary, BoundaryNormal(mesh));
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - `mesh.displace(...)` — deform the mesh by a vector field
  - `F::x` — coordinate projection function (see @ref guides-core-concepts)
  - @ref Rodin::Variational::BoundaryNormal "BoundaryNormal(mesh)" — outward
    unit normal vector on the boundary
  - `gf.project(Region::Boundary, ...)` — project a field only on the boundary

  @subsection examples-index-geometry-partition Mesh Partitioning (Scotch)

  **File:** `examples/Scotch/MeshPartitioner.cpp`

  Partitions a mesh into 16 parts using the Scotch graph partitioner:
  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {32, 32});
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(2, 2);

  Scotch::Partitioner partitioner(mesh);
  partitioner.partition(16);

  for (auto it = mesh.getCell(); it; ++it)
    mesh.setAttribute({2, it->getIndex()}, partitioner.getPartition(it->getIndex()) + 1);
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - `mesh.getConnectivity().compute(2, 2)` — cell-to-cell adjacency required
    by the partitioner
  - `Scotch::Partitioner` — wrapper around the SCOTCH graph partitioner
  - `mesh.setAttribute(...)` — label cells by partition for visualization

  - @subpage examples-geometry-index

  @section examples-index-mmg Working with MMG

  @subsection examples-index-mmg-optimize Mesh Optimization

  **File:** `examples/MMG/MeshOptimization.cpp`

  Optimizes a coarse uniform mesh using the MMG remesher:
  @code{.cpp}
  MMG::Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, { n, n });

  mesh.setCorner(0);   // pin corner vertices
  mesh.setCorner(15);

  for (auto it = mesh.getBoundary(); !it.end(); ++it)
    mesh.setRidge(it->getIndex()); // mark boundary edges as ridges

  MMG::Optimizer().setHMax(0.5).optimize(mesh);
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - @ref Rodin::External::MMG::Mesh "MMG::Mesh" — extended mesh type
    compatible with the MMG library
  - `setCorner()` / `setRidge()` — geometric feature preservation
  - @ref Rodin::External::MMG::Optimizer "MMG::Optimizer" with fluent API
    (`setHMax`, `setHMin`, `setHausdorff`, `setAngleDetection`)

  @subsection examples-index-mmg-levelset Implicit Domain Meshing

  **File:** `examples/MMG/DomainDiscretization.cpp`

  Creates a mesh of a circle from a level-set function:
  @code{.cpp}
  MMG::Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, { n, n });
  mesh.scale(1. / (n - 1));

  P1 fes(mesh);
  MMG::RealGridFunction gf(fes);
  gf = [](const Geometry::Point& p)
       {
         return (p.x() - 0.5) * (p.x() - 0.5)
              + (p.y() - 0.5) * (p.y() - 0.5) - radius;
       };

  MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer().split(interior, { interior, exterior })
                             .setBoundaryReference(boundary)
                             .setHMax(hmax)
                             .discretize(gf);
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - `MMG::RealGridFunction` — grid function type required by MMG operations
  - @ref Rodin::External::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "LevelSetDiscretizer" —
    splits regions along the zero level set of a function
  - `.split(from, {interior, exterior})` — defines how regions are divided
  - `.setBoundaryReference(attr)` — the attribute assigned to the new interface
  - The discretized mesh conforms exactly to the zero-isosurface

  - @subpage examples-mmg-index

  @section examples-index-solid Solid Mechanics

  The @ref Rodin::Solid "Solid" module provides hyperelastic constitutive laws
  and nonlinear integrators for large-deformation solid mechanics, while the
  @ref Rodin::Variational::LinearElasticityIntegral "LinearElasticityIntegral"
  covers the small-strain (linearized) case.

  @subsection examples-index-solid-blockgravity Block Under Gravity (NeoHookean)

  **File:** `examples/Solid/BlockGravity.cpp`

  **Problem:** A unit square block, fixed on its bottom edge, is loaded by a
  time-varying gravitational body force. A compressible NeoHookean material
  model is used with Newton linearization for the nonlinear problem.

  **Key code:**
  @code{.cpp}
  // Material
  const Real lambda = E * nu / ((1.0 + nu) * (1.0 - 2.0 * nu));
  const Real mu     = E / (2.0 * (1.0 + nu));
  Solid::NeoHookean law(lambda, mu);

  // Solution field
  GridFunction u(Vh);
  u = VectorFunction{ Zero(), Zero() };

  // At each quasi-static load step:
  Solid::MaterialTangent tangent(law, du, v);
  tangent.setDisplacement(u);

  Solid::InternalForce residual(law, v);
  residual.setDisplacement(u);

  Problem newton(du, v);
  newton = tangent
         + residual
         - Integral(bodyForce, v)
         + DirichletBC(du, zero).on(bottomBC);

  SparseLU linearSolver(newton);
  NewtonSolver solver(linearSolver);
  solver.setMaxIterations(50)
        .setAbsoluteTolerance(1e-10)
        .setRelativeTolerance(1e-8);
  solver.solve(u);
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - @ref Rodin::Solid::NeoHookean "Solid::NeoHookean" — compressible
    NeoHookean constitutive law parameterized by @f$ (\lambda, \mu) @f$
  - @ref Rodin::Solid::MaterialTangent "Solid::MaterialTangent" — linearized
    tangent operator @f$ K[\delta u] = \partial F_{\text{int}} / \partial u @f$;
    use `.setDisplacement(u)` to set the current deformation state
  - @ref Rodin::Solid::InternalForce "Solid::InternalForce" — internal force
    residual @f$ F_{\text{int}}(u; v) @f$; also uses `.setDisplacement(u)`
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::NewtonSolver "NewtonSolver" — wraps a linear solver
    for Newton-Raphson iteration (defaults: max 100 iterations, atol 1e-12,
    rtol 1e-8)
  - `solver.solve(u)` — Newton iteration updates `u` in place
  - XDMF time-series output: `xdmf.write(static_cast<Real>(step))`
  - Boundary labeling by iterating over boundary edges and checking coordinates

  @subsection examples-index-solid-cantilever Transient Cantilever Beam (Newmark)

  **File:** `examples/Solid/CantileverBeam.cpp`

  **Problem:** A rectangular beam (4×1) clamped on the left, subject to
  time-varying traction on the right. Models inertia, viscous damping, and
  NeoHookean hyperelasticity with Newmark-beta time integration
  (@f$ \beta = 1/4 @f$, @f$ \gamma = 1/2 @f$ — average acceleration).

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - Newmark-beta time integration with predictor-corrector scheme
  - Combined inertia (`aMass * Integral(du, w)`), damping
    (`aDamp * Integral(du, w)`), and hyperelastic internal force
  - `DirichletBC(du, zero).on(leftBC)` — BC on the Newton increment
  - Discrete derivative check for verifying tangent consistency
  - `solver.setDampingFactor(1.0)` — full Newton step (no line search damping)
  - `xdmf.write(t).flush()` — time-stamped output with flush
  - `xdmf.close()` — finalize the XDMF file

  @section examples-index-shapeoptimization Shape Optimization

  @subsection examples-index-shapeopt-cantilever Level-Set Cantilever 2D

  **File:** `examples/ShapeOptimization/LevelSetCantilever2D.cpp`

  **Problem:** Compliance minimization with volume penalty for a 2D cantilever.
  The domain is represented as a level set, and the optimization loop combines:
  1. MMG mesh optimization,
  2. domain trimming (remove exterior),
  3. elasticity state solve,
  4. Hilbert-regularized shape gradient computation,
  5. signed-distance reconstruction (Eikonal),
  6. semi-Lagrangian level-set advection,
  7. implicit-domain reconstruction (MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer),
  8. XDMF visualization.

  **Optimization loop (simplified):**
  @code{.cpp}
  while (i < maxIt)
  {
    // 1. Optimize mesh
    MMG::Optimizer().setHMax(hmax).setHMin(hmin).setHausdorff(hausd).optimize(th);

    // 2. Trim exterior
    SubMesh trimmed = th.trim(exterior);

    // 3. Solve elasticity
    Problem elasticity(u, v);
    elasticity = LinearElasticityIntegral(u, v)(lambda, mu)
               - BoundaryIntegral(f, v).over(GammaN)
               + DirichletBC(u, VectorFunction{0, 0}).on(GammaD);
    Solver::CG(elasticity).solve();

    // 4. Hilbert shape gradient
    auto jac = Jacobian(u.getSolution());
    jac.traceOf(interior);
    auto e = 0.5 * (jac + jac.T());
    auto Ae = 2.0 * mu * e + lambda * Trace(e) * IdentityMatrix(d);
    auto n = FaceNormal(th);
    n.traceOf(interior);

    Problem hilbert(g, w);
    hilbert = Integral(alpha * alpha * Jacobian(g), Jacobian(w))
            + Integral(g, w)
            - FaceIntegral(Dot(Ae, e) - ell, Dot(n, w)).over(Gamma);
    Solver::CG(hilbert).solve();

    // 5. Distance
    Distance::Eikonal(dist).setInterior(interior).setInterface(Gamma).solve().sign();

    // 6. Advect level set
    Advection::Lagrangian(advect, test, dist, dJ).step(dt);

    // 7. Reconstruct domain
    th = MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer().split(interior, {interior, exterior})
                                   .setBoundaryReference(Gamma)
                                   .discretize(advect.getSolution());

    // 8. XDMF
    xdmf.write(i).flush();
  }
  @endcode

  **Features demonstrated:**
  - @ref Rodin::External::MMG::Optimizer "MMG::Optimizer" and
    @ref Rodin::External::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer"
    for adaptive remeshing and implicit-domain reconstruction
  - `th.trim(exterior)` — extract a subdomain as a @ref Rodin::Geometry::SubMesh
    "SubMesh"
  - `Jacobian(u).traceOf(interior)` — evaluate the Jacobian from the interior
    side of an interface
  - `FaceNormal(th).traceOf(interior)` — face normal evaluated from the
    interior side
  - `FaceIntegral(integrand, test).over(Gamma)` — integration over interior
    faces
  - `Trace(e)`, `IdentityMatrix(d)`, `Dot(Ae, e)` — tensor operators
  - @ref Rodin::Distance::Eikonal "Distance::Eikonal" — signed-distance
    function via Fast Marching Method
  - @ref Rodin::Advection::Lagrangian "Advection::Lagrangian" —
    semi-Lagrangian advection with `step(dt)`
  - `Frobenius(dJ)` for computing the norm of the gradient field
  - Named XDMF grids: `xdmf.grid("domain")` and `xdmf.grid("state")` with
    `IO::XDMF::MeshPolicy::Transient` for evolving meshes
  - Error recovery: `catch (const Alert::Exception& e)` with `hmax /= 2`
    retry strategy

  @subsection examples-index-shapeopt-simple Simple Cantilever 2D

  **File:** `examples/ShapeOptimization/SimpleCantilever2D.cpp`

  A simplified shape optimization workflow without level sets. Uses:
  - `BilinearForm bf(u, v)` with `LinearElasticityIntegral(u, v)(lambda, mu)`
    for computing compliance `bf(w, w)`
  - Similar Hilbert-regularized gradient approach

